WEBVTT

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:06.610
Well then, we’ll begin the awards ceremony for the 39th Pantasia newcomers’ battle.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.020
In third place, we have
Suwabara Kai from the Main Branch.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.240
Also in third place, we have Kawachi
Kyousuke from the Southern Tokyo Branch.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.200
This is cramped.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.160
Why don’t you just step down?

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:19.990
Why don’t <i>you</i> step down?

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.500
In second place, we have Kanmuri
Shigeru from the Shinjuku Central Branch.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.500
And, the champion is...

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Azuma Kazuma from the Southern Tokyo Branch.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.300
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.260
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.510
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.980
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:50.190
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.820
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:11.140
Somewhere far away, there are people listening carefully.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:19.110
In every place, there are people looking up at the sky.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:26.940
Under the night sky, we, who are whistling...

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:34.700
without words, are just tracing constellations with our fingers.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:42.170
The dreams of the cold-sensitive... Your cold hands...

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:51.550
The warming magic is to believe in one path.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:02:00.310
The single star found on the other side of the comet cloud...

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.570
whether it be a shining or a faint star...

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.520
is the light of only you.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.310
On the other side of the cloud in your heart,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:16.120
is a sign post that remains unseen.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:23.840
Now, opening these hands, what will you believe in today?

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.160
Let’s all make bread together!!

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.840
It’s <i>Japan</i> #2!

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.660
To continue,
we’ll distribute everyone’s prizes

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.300
and the catalog for the extra prize: studying abroad in France.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.470
Congratulations.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:45.930
Thank you.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.480
Kawachi.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:50.930
Well done.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:51.980
Take it.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.020
T-Thank you.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.240
Prize money of 300,000 yen,
and studying abroad in France.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.030
It’s true that I was aiming for the championship, but...

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:05.500
for someone as poor as me, the 300,000
yen prize for third place is a lot of money.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.540
Thank you.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.670
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8...

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.920
To look inside right here...

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.470
He has no sense of refinement.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.010
There’s only 150,000 yen here.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.140
Kuro-yan, there’s only half of the prize money here.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.270
Of course.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.070
There were two third place winners, after all.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.560
More importantly,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.060
if you say Kuro-yan one more time,
I’ll bring it down to 75,000 yen!

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.560
When there are two people at the same position,

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.600
both the prize money and the extra prize shall be halved.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.690
Well, we can’t really halve the extra prize,

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.070
so the studying abroad in France prize will be
given to the Main Branch employee Suwabara.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.990
And the right to work at the Main
Branch plus the fourth place prize,

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.030
a 2000 yen Pantasia collector’s
card, will be given to Kawachi.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.080
This is how we decided to divide it in half.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.090
Since Suwabara’s already a Main Branch
employee, he can’t be given that right, but...

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:00.380
I feel that I’ve really been let down here.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:02.600
And what the hell’s with this collector’s card?

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.840
It’s a really annoying design.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.140
Why’s it Yukino?

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.810
Kawachi-san.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:10.480
What is it?

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.560
Take it, please.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:16.810
W-What are you trying to pull here?

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.320
There’s no deep meaning to it.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.020
I’ve already lived in France for about two years.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.660
It’s not a country
that I particularly like, so...

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:28.740
Are you sure, Shigeru?

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.330
Yes.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.160
It might not be appropriate to ask for this in exchange,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.040
but later on, I’ll have just a little request of you.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.210
All right.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.050
Well then... next,

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:42.220
let’s have those wishing to join
the Main Branch step forward.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.060
Kawachi...

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:00.390
Azuma...

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.240
You’re not going to join?

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.740
Kawachi and Shigeru, eh?

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:11.040
Azuma.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:12.410
Are you sure?

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:13.290
Yeah...

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.330
I won’t go.

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:17.200
I see.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:17.870
Well then...

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.340
I won’t go to the Main Branch either.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:22.050
I wish to...

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.550
apply for employment at the Southern Tokyo Branch.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.590
You’re going to go to the
Southern Tokyo Branch, you say?

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:32.970
That damn brat.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.770
What’s he up to?

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:37.190
It shouldn’t matter, right?

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:38.390
Because...

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.770
strictly speaking, the right to
go to the Main Branch means

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:45.770
you also have the right to
transfer to any branch within Pantasia.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.410
It said so in the rules of the newcomers’ battle.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:50.530
Of course it doesn’t matter.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:51.400
However...

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.280
the Manager’s permission is required at the very least.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:55.800
That’s a big OK!

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.750
There’s nothing more we could ask for.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:00.790
M-Manager...

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.420
It suits us just fine.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.460
We’ll have a vacant position, after all...

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:07.500
Also, he’s the runner up of the newcomers’ battle.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.470
What could we possibly complain about?

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.430
I need to get a lot of things done,

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.180
so I’ll join you at the Southern
Tokyo Branch in a week from now.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.440
Let’s all get along, shall we?

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.440
S-Sure.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.200
Yeah... Let’s...

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:26.950
In that case, I’ll also join the Main
Branch in a week from now...

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:28.200
All right.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:31.070
In that case, with everything done,

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.160
we now conclude the Pantasia newcomers’ battle.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:34.870
Disperse!

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:46.050
Kirisaki-kun, what do you think of the results this time?

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:50.880
The newcomers’ battle this time had
all three Pantasia successor candidates,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.680
Yukino, Tsukino, and Mizuno
involved in a competition for the first time.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.140
As the manager and leader, who do
you think is the most noteworthy?

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:04.020
Could you tell me frankly what you think?

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:09.570
Both the first and third place winners
came from the Southern Tokyo Branch.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:14.240
Also, the runner up decided to
transfer to the Southern Tokyo Branch.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.040
I’m sure that you can tell
which is the most noteworthy...

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:20.580
without having to ask for my opinion.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.170
That’s right, Grandfather.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.840
There’s nothing to be uncertain of here.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:29.920
Yukino.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:31.960
Tsukino has an eye for recognizing a good artisan.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.550
She has a gentle personality,

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.050
and, more than anything,

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.520
she’s a flawless child who’s beloved by everyone.

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.770
I’m really proud of her as her older sister.

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:48.280
That’s why I encouraged our
Kanmuri to work at her place.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:53.400
He’ll lose out as a human being if he’s
just working in his laboratory all the time.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.610
And in order to help his new discoveries as well...

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.030
Is that so?

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:57.790
Yes.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.540
However, Tsukino’s still a high school student.

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.950
She’s too young to be the successor.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.790
For now, I’m thinking of playing a supporting role

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:11.550
to help her support Pantasia, but...

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.350
What do you think, Grandfather?

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:16.760
You are...

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.050
a really kind child, aren’t you?

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.350
Yukino-san.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.060
The person in this photograph...

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.940
Do you know the SP named Ishiyama Tomozou?

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.020
Hello, Mother?

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:32.570
It’s me... Me.

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:33.230
What?

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:34.690
I’m not trying to pull the “It’s me” scam on you!

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:35.490
It’s Tomozou!

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:37.150
Mother...

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:38.740
don’t look for me.

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:41.170
If I’m caught, I won’t be let off easy.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.420
What about that person?

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.370
At present, his whereabouts are unknown.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:48.540
Oh, my!

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:49.920
At the semifinals,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.800
enzymes called endoproteases
were added to Kawachi-kun’s flour...

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.850
This is the SP who is
suspected to have sabotaged his work.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.230
Oh my, how horrible!

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:05.350
But, I don’t know any SP by that name.

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:09.610
Oh, but I thought that this SP was affiliated to you...

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.110
I see...

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:14.070
If I ever meet him,

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:15.950
I’ll put him into a grinding machine...

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:17.450
the first thing I’ll do

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.580
And grind him to pieces...

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.830
is contact you.

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:25.620
And spread him over Tokyo Bay as fish food.

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:28.210
Once I do that, you won’t be able to find him, will you?

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:29.460
You idiot.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.510
L-Like I said, it’s me. Your son, me.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.010
This isn’t an “It’s me” scam!

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:45.010
I’m really sorry.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.890
The Tsukino flavored
<i>Japan</i> #57 is all sold out.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:49.350
Welcome!

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:50.440
Thank you!

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:54.190
Why is it only me who has to work so hard?

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:56.780
Yes.

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:58.950
This is Pantasia’s Southern Tokyo Branch.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:00.610
This is Kirisaki.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:02.450
May I speak to Matsushiro-san?

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.790
M-Meister Kirisaki?

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:07.160
Oh, the bird dude, eh?

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:09.250
Please hold on for a moment.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:10.170
Manager!

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:13.580
It’s me.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.040
About Yukino, right?

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:15.670
Yes.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.000
It was no use.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.880
She is as slippery as an eel,
and has escaped.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.050
I see.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:24.680
She’s one shrewd woman as usual.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:28.010
I’m sorry, Kawachi.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.310
We can’t seem to lay anything on Yukino.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:33.070
Oh, don’t worry about that anymore.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.230
I’m not worried about it at all.

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:36.410
Is that so?

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:37.690
In that case...

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.200
Don’t be slacking, and get to work!

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:44.870
I mean, you can’t stay here
for more than a week, right?!

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:46.410
Don’t be fooling around!

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:47.700
Okay!

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.250
But, I’m still kinda curious...

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:54.460
About what?

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:56.750
About that Meister Kirisaki.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:58.720
Who in the world is that dude?

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:03.220
What kind of face do you think
he’s hiding underneath that mask?

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:05.890
I’m sure he’s a guy who likes birds.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:07.220
No, that’s not what I meant...

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:08.680
Come to think of it...

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:12.810
Manager told me that he was selling bread by performing roadside tricks in France.

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:13.940
Isn’t that right?

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:16.730
He’s really a mysterious old dude.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.740
You guys!

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:28.170
Ow ow ow...

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:31.490
Are you all that curious about Meister Kirisaki?

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:33.160
Not anymore...

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.040
I’m sorry.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:35.500
I’m really not anymore...

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:36.800
Well, I suppose it’s all right.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.010
It’ll be a problem if you’re too curious about it to work.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.380
The four of them are playing around again.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:44.010
Poor little me...

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.390
Please look to your left.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.350
Oh, yes.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:53.020
We see Pantasia’s greatest rival,
St. Pierre’s Main Branch, right?

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.770
The owner there is actually...

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.990
Meister Kirisaki’s father!

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:01.240
What was that?!

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:02.780
What? No way!

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.280
It’s the truth.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:08.030
St. Pierre’s owner Kirisaki Ryuuichi,
when he was young,

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:10.950
fell in love with a local girl while training in France.

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:15.080
And so, Meister Kirisaki popped out into the world.

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:16.300
For real...?

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:20.630
B-But...

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.510
why would Manager know such a thing?

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:27.090
A long time ago, Kirisaki told me this himself.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:30.350
It’s from when I was working
with him at the Main Branch.

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:35.390
What did you say?!

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:36.980
Is that true?

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:39.240
Yes. It’s the truth.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:42.940
He said that he would make an
original, Japanese bread in Japan.

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:45.990
He departed, leaving me and
my little sister behind in France.

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:50.420
I do not know if he was successful or not,

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:53.290
but he became a prominent man in Japan.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:58.460
If that’s so, shouldn’t you go to St. Pierre,
the symbol of his accomplishment?

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.500
Perhaps rely on your father?

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:01.840
I am...

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:05.010
not that weak of a human being.

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:09.430
But, why tell me something like this...

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:10.680
I wonder...

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.520
By telling somebody...

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:19.150
I might have wanted to
prepare for throwing myself away...

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.790
That was the last time I saw his face.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.540
He came to Japan to make an original bread, did he?

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:40.170
The old man I met ten years ago
also said the same thing, didn’t he?

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:44.590
I wonder if that old man succeeded?

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.490
Already morning, eh?

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.280
I can’t get rid of the fatigue from the newcomers’ battle.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:11.070
What?

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:13.700
Azuma made some rice, did he?

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.250
I guess even morons can be thoughtful...

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:18.790
I’d been seeing nothing but bread
all through the newcomers’ battle.

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:21.170
I’m starting to want to eat rice.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:23.790
Let’s see...

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:25.260
Is the rice all nicely done...?

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:30.000
Wha...

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.180
What’s this?!

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.020
A rice cooker <i>Japan</i>, is it?

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:41.100
Well, I suppose it sounds like
something Azuma-san would make.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.520
So, how does it taste?

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:46.060
I’m not gonna put something
suspicious like that into my mouth!

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:47.520
That’s mean!
It’s really good...

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:51.190
A rice cooker only reaches temperatures of 110°C.

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:54.530
If you bake it at those temperatures,
the dough’s going to stay raw for sure.

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:55.990
That’s not true.

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:57.370
It’s baked properly, I tell you.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.790
Be quiet and work, you idiot.

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:02.290
That goes for you as well!

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.550
O-Okay...

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:07.550
Shouldn’t that be for all of you?!

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:23.060
Can I get <i>Japan</i> #57... please?

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:28.570
T-Tsukino-san!

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:30.880
Is something wrong?

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:33.070
This child wants #57...

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:36.080
<i>Japan</i> #57 costs 0 yen, right?

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:37.820
is 0 yen.

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:41.330
0 yen? 0 yen?! Nothing?!

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:43.130
I’m sorry, kid.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:46.120
There’s no <i>Japan</i> #57 today.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.290
What?!

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:50.340
It got completely sold out yesterday.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:52.050
Then, make some please!

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:56.050
We can’t make that unless
there’s leftovers that we haven’t sold.

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:56.810
I’m sorry.

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:58.810
Oh, it’s the <i>Japan</i> person!

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:01.310
Oh, you know him?

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:03.810
I saw him on TV.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:04.770
Is that so?

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.350
You saw the broadcast that time?

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:09.810
Boy, how did I look on TV?

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:12.820
Are you an impersonator of
the man with the disheveled hair?

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:13.580
The Real Thing

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:18.290
Hey, <i>Japan</i> person...

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:20.240
Can I eat a <i>Japan</i>?

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.040
I really want to eat one.

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:23.680
Yeah, no problem.

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:25.330
I don’t have #57,

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.170
but I have a really delicious one.

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:28.120
Really?

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:29.130
Yeah!

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:30.970
Made from a rice cooker...

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:33.930
<i>Japan</i>... #2!

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:35.550
A rice cooker?

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:41.310
Wait up!

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:44.270
You can’t give something like that to a customer!

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:47.060
It could blow up into something big if he
hurts his stomach and his parents sue us!

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:50.520
It’s all right.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:51.780
There’s nothing to worry about.

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:56.570
Wow!

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:02.080
Eat it and see.

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.800
Delicious!

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:13.960
Mister, this is really delicious!

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:14.930
Isn’t it?

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:17.220
This thing is...?

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:22.060
This... This is...?!

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:33.780
Rice cooker <i>Japan</i>.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:37.900
The raw materials are that of bread,
but the insides are sticky and soft, like rice.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:41.280
This is the taste you’d only get from a rice cooker.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:44.330
Fluffy fluffy fluffy.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:45.870
Rice cooker <i>Japan</i>.

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.340
Fluffy fluffy fluffy.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:49.000
What?!

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:50.420
Why is this?

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:52.250
Normally, it should be undercooked.

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.930
But, the insides have swelled
up nicely after being heated.

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:58.300
Fluffy fluffy fluffy fluffy fluffy.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:03.550
The temperature inside a
bread oven is approximately 180°C.

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:06.810
But, a rice cooker doesn’t go above 110°C.

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:09.850
It should remain undercooked.

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:10.750
So, why?!

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:11.990
It’s simple!

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.320
Fluffy fluffy fluffy.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:17.570
Manager...

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:21.740
In a bread oven, the bread is
heated via convectional currents of heat.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:23.990
In a rice cooker, the heated
surface is directly touching the dough.

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:25.620
So, the heat reaches it more easily.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:26.660
I see!

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:30.000
That’s why it wasn’t
undercooked, and was baked nicely.

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.170
Fluffy fluffy fluffy.

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.470
Fluffy fluffy fluffy fluffy.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:41.430
Since it’s made in a rice cooker, I thought
that even I could make it at home, but...

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:45.020
a lot of difficult words came up and...

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:47.600
If looks like it’d be impossible for me.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:50.900
That’s not true.

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:52.640
Because...

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:57.030
I made <i>Japan</i> #2 by myself for the
first time when I was six years old.

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.280
Six years old?!
That’s the same age as me!

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:00.530
Also...

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:04.320
Even the Yakitate Japan program producers can make it.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:05.120
Really?

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:07.830
Then, even a six year old like myself could make it!

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:09.040
This is how you make it.

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:12.040
Does everyone have some paper ready?

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:13.670
First, we’ll start with the ingredients.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:15.670
350g of bread flour,

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:17.340
21g of butter,

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.130
21g of sugar,

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:21.010
35cc of milk,

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:22.760
180cc of water,

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.380
5g of dry yeast,

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:26.220
and 6.5g of salt.

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:30.310
The most important thing in baking bread
is to measure out the ingredients properly.

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:32.360
The flour.

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:34.570
Sugar.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:36.650
Salt.

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:39.450
Yeast, dissolved in water.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:41.330
Water.

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:44.910
Add milk, and...

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:46.660
mix it.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.630
Then, add the butter.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:58.880
Knead it some more, thoroughly.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:02.600
When if no longer feels
sticky, shape it so that it’s round.

00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:05.300
Leave it in a warm place for 60 minutes.

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:06.770
This is the primary fermentation.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:09.980
And then, it’ll rise like this.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.610
Next, you get rid of the extra gas.

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:15.390
Don’t grind against it with your fist.

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:18.570
All you need to do is drop it from a height of 50cm.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.270
Once you’ve released the gas,
start the secondary fermentation.

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:23.580
Yup, you leave it for 60 minutes.

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:24.650
And then...

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:26.250
switch on the button on the rice cooker.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:28.450
And now, you’re finally baking it!

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:31.700
After about 60 minutes in the rice cooker,
one side will be baked.

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:33.920
Turn it over, and cook it again.

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:42.010
Then, once that’s done, turn it over
once more and cook it once again.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:46.270
Once you’ve cooked it 3 times in total, you’re done!

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:53.440
Just a little work, and you can have a
freshly baked, fluffy fluffy <i>Japan</i> #2.

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:55.680
It takes a little time,

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.460
but if you want to eat a delicious and fresh bread,

00:20:58.460 --> 00:21:00.400
something like this isn’t very much, right?

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:05.990
Adding eggs or green tea to suit
your taste isn’t a bad idea, either.

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:08.530
It’s really and absolutely delicious,

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:10.660
so everyone, try challenging it and see!

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:13.700
Well, it’s easy, right?

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:14.540
Yeah!

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:18.750
Even the Yakitate Japan program
producers can make it after all...

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:19.920
That’s right.

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:23.930
The two children are frolicking.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:25.010
But...

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:29.680
are the Yakitate Japan program producers all that bad?

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:31.600
Yeah, they’re really bad.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:37.100
If was really delicious, <i>Japan</i> person!

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:37.980
Yeah!

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:39.310
See ya.

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:40.610
See ya!

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:43.110
Bye bye!

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:47.570
Oh, that’s right.

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:49.610
When I grow up,

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:52.330
I’ll become a baker who won’t lose to you!

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:01.500
Yeah!

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.510
I won’t lose either!

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:10.020
I’ll make a <i>Japan</i> that won’t lose to the old man, either.

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:14.230
Someday...

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:16.310
Definitely.

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:20.810
So, it finally begins.

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:24.320
The real extermination of the cockroaches.

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:27.400
Right?

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:29.570
Mr. Owner of St. Pierre...

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:56.240
On this chest, big and strong

00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:58.360
A look that glitters and doesn't lose.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.360
That's what I searched for, fumbling in the wind.

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:01.660
The answer for now is...

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:04.580
For now, I'll watch the situation and wait for a chance.
I got the patience to wait at a distance.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.370
A prisoner who just screams is no good.

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:08.160
Just Follow Me! I'll lend you my shoulder occasionally.

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:18.260
It's by no means a fleeting dream.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:21.140
Let this sentiment reach you. Heart to Heart, Soul to Soul.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:23.180
The seasons turn. Splendid as a spring storm.

00:23:23.180 --> 00:23:24.590
Win Big is the motto.

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:29.390
Does a thing like eternity really exist?

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:31.230
And so we... C'mon.

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:36.780
Yeah, yeah, we live to touch the one shining moment.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:57.590
To all the soldiers fighting today using the tail wind...

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:08.290
Finally, even I start working at
the Main Branch of my dreams.

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:10.300
Man, it’s gonna get lonely.

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:11.550
Yes, it will.

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:12.300
Yeah!

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:14.340
What do you mean, “pfft”?

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:16.140
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:17.140
The ten billion yen man?!

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:18.640
Pantasia’s life or death crisis!

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:19.970
Be sure to watch!

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:22.730
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:24.270
Making bread in a rice cooker!

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.060
Everyone, do you think you can do it well?

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:27.350
If you like soft bread,

00:24:27.350 --> 00:24:29.400
then add extra water or milk.

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:31.740
Let’s all try making an original <i>Japan</i>!

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:32.660
Yeah, yeah!
